course: everyday
lesson: 222
title: Phones, visiting friends, presents and thank-you notes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On the bus or in a shop you speak quietly and keep a call short, and you let a parent answer calls or messages from numbers you do not know, because they could be scammers.
explain: At a friend's house you stay in the rooms you are invited into, help tidy up before you leave, and if you break something you tell the adult at once and say sorry.
explain: Presents are about kindness, not money: you thank everyone, never compare prices, and protect the giver's feelings, for example when you get the same present twice.
explain: A short thank-you note or card to each giver within a week or two shows real thanks; a message is fine for small things, but a handwritten card means more for big gifts, and days out are gifts too.
explain: A girl in Vancouver gets the same book from two aunts; she thanks both warmly, and later, with her mum's help and never in front of the givers, swaps one for a different book.
fact: On a phone in a shop or on the bus, you speak quietly and keep it short.
fact: You let a parent answer, or ignore, calls and messages from unknown numbers, because they could be scammers.
fact: At a friend's house you stay in the rooms you are invited into.
fact: You help tidy up any mess you made before you go home.
fact: If you break something at a friend's home, you tell the adult straight away and say sorry.
fact: If you get the same present twice, you thank both givers and may exchange one later with your parents' help, never in front of the giver.
fact: When you get money or a gift card, you say thank you and may tell the giver what you will buy.
fact: When a present comes by post, you phone, message or write soon to say it arrived and that you liked it.
fact: You never compare the prices of presents, and a small or homemade present is fine.
fact: You write a short thank-you note to each giver within a week or two.
fact: A trip or day out is a gift too, so you thank the person and mention the best part.
fact: A message is fine for small things, but a handwritten card means more for big gifts.
exercise: Your gran calls you while you are on a busy bus. What do you do? | Speak quietly and keep it short | Talk loudly so she can hear over the bus | Put her on speaker so everyone hears | Chat for as long as you both like
exercise: Why should you speak quietly on the phone in a shop? | Other people do not want to hear your call | Phones work much better and clearer when you speak quietly | Shops do not allow phones at all | The call costs more if you are loud
exercise: Your phone rings with a number you do not know. What do you do? | Let a parent answer, or ignore it | Answer and give your name and address | Call it back many times to find out | Answer and do whatever the caller says
exercise: Why can calls from unknown numbers be risky? | The caller could be a scammer | Unknown numbers cost more to answer | They always bring bad news with them | They can break the phone if you answer
exercise: At your friend's house you are curious about her parents' bedroom. What do you do? | Stay in the rooms you are invited into | Have a quick look while they are busy | Open the cupboards to see what is inside | Ask your friend to show you secretly
exercise: You and your friend made a big Lego mess in his room. It is time to go. What do you do? | Help tidy up before you leave | Leave, since it is his room and his mess | Hide the pieces under the bed quickly | Tidy only the pieces that you used
exercise: You knock over and break a vase at a friend's house. What do you do? | Tell the adult straight away and say sorry | Hide the pieces carefully behind the sofa for later | Blame the dog | Say nothing and go home early
exercise: Two cousins give you the same board game. What do you do? | Thank both, and maybe exchange one later with your parents | Tell the second cousin straight away that you already have that game | Give one back in front of everyone | Thank only the one who gave it first
exercise: Your uncle gives you a gift card. What is a nice thing to say? | "Thank you! I'm going to buy a new book with it" | "How much is on it?" | "I'd prefer a real present" | Nothing, as it is only a card
exercise: A parcel arrives from your grandpa in Wales. What do you do? | Phone, message or write soon to say thank you | Wait until you see him next year and thank him then | Thank him only if he asks | Send it back to show you got it
exercise: Your friend's family has little money and she gives you a homemade bracelet. What do you do? | Thank her warmly and never compare prices | Tell her what all the others gave you for your birthday | Say you will wear it only at home | Ask where she bought it
exercise: After your birthday, when should thank-you notes be written? | Within a week or two | Only at the end of the year | Never, as you said thanks at the party | Only to people who gave expensive things
exercise: Your aunt took you to the aquarium. What makes the best thank-you note? | Thanking her and mentioning the best part, like the sharks | Just writing "Thanks" | A note listing what you did not like | Asking for another trip in the note
exercise: Your grandparents gave you a new bike. How should you thank them? | A handwritten card, as it is a big gift | Just a quick emoji message | Nothing, as family does not need thanks | A note from your parents instead
test: Which is good phone manners on a train? | A short, quiet call | A long video call on loudspeaker | Shouting so the other person hears | Playing music during the call
test: A message arrives from an unknown number saying you have won a prize. What should you do? | Show a parent and do not reply | Reply with your address to get it | Click the link at once | Forward it to all your friends
test: Ali is at a friend's house and wants to open the fridge for a drink. What is better? | Ask the adult or his friend first | Help himself, as friends share | Open every cupboard to find a cup | Wait until nobody is looking
test: A boy in Sydney spilled juice on his friend's carpet. What should he do? | Tell the adult straight away and say sorry | Cover the wet patch with a cushion so nobody sees it | Leave quickly before it is found | Blame his friend
test: You get two identical jumpers for Christmas. Which is kind? | Thanking both and swapping one later, not in front of them | Saying "I already have this one" | Giving one back on the spot | Asking which one was cheaper
test: Why do givers like to hear that a posted present arrived? | They want to know it arrived and that you liked it | They need to know so that they can pay the post office | They want it sent back | They forget what they sent
test: Which is true about presents? | They are about kindness, not money | The more expensive, the better the friend | Homemade gifts are not real presents | You should tell people what the prices were
test: Which of these is a gift you should also write a thank-you note for? | A day out at the zoo with your aunt | A glass of water at a friend's house | Someone holding a door for you | Passing the salt at dinner
test: Why does a handwritten card mean more than a quick message? | It takes effort and people keep it | It is much faster to write than a text | It is cheaper than a message | It arrives sooner
test: got 2 of the same lego set for my bday lol | Thank both givers; maybe swap one later, never in front of them | Tell the second giver it's a repeat | Give one back at the party | Say "I already have this"
test: unknown number keeps calling me | Let a parent answer or ignore it | Answer it and give them your full name | Call it back straight away | Text them your address
test: nan posted me a present, it came today | Phone, message or write soon to say it arrived and you liked it | Wait until Christmas to mention it | Say nothing, she knows | Ask what it cost
test: on the bus, mum rings | Speak quietly and keep it short | Put it on loudspeaker so you can both hear | Shout so she can hear | Talk as long as you want
explain: A girl in Florida answered a call from an unknown number and told the caller her parents were away; the caller was a scammer, and the family had to change all their bank details.
explain: A boy in Ontario went to a friend's house without telling his mum; when he was not home by dark she called the police, who searched the streets for him.
fact: Scammers phone children to find out when a house is empty or to get money and passwords.
fact: When nobody knows where a child is, adults lose precious time if something has really gone wrong.
fact: «Got together» means two people started being a couple, and a card or message with hearts for «someone special» is usually for someone a person loves or goes out with; it is their private, happy secret.
test: some1 on the phone asking if my parents r home | Don't say they're out; take a message or get an adult | Say they're out till 9 | Tell them your address | Hang up and call them back later
test: Why must Kai tell his mum before going to a friend's house? | So she knows where he is if something goes wrong | So she can buy a present | Mums like knowing everything | It is only a rule for weekends
test: caller says i won a prize, just need mum's card number | It's a scam, hang up and tell an adult | Read the number out quickly so you get the prize | Ask for the prize first | Give half the number
test: did u hear some scammer rang a kid and asked when his parents go to work and then they got robbed? real lesson? | Let a parent handle calls from unknown numbers | Answer politely and tell them the times if they ask nicely | Only answer if they say they're the bank | It only happens to rich families
test: apparently that girl got the same Lego set twice for her birthday and told her aunty to her face it was rubbish. what's the lesson? | Thank both givers; swap one later, never in front of them | Tell the giver straight away it's a double, so they can change it | Give the second one back at the party | Keep both and say nothing at all
test: my mate's saying that kid only gave a homemade card at the party cos his family's poor. should u spread it? | No, homemade gifts are fine and that gossip is mean | Yes, it's just what happened | Yes, so people know why | Yes, if you don't say his name
test: you see a text on mum's phone from "R" with loads of hearts and mum says "that's private, love". what's right? | Accept it, don't read her messages, she can tell you when she's ready | Grab the phone and read it all | Text R back and ask who they are | Tell everyone at school mum has a secret
test: big sis is making a card with hearts for "someone special, don't you DARE tell" — what is it and what's right? | It's probably for someone she likes or goes out with; keep her happy secret | It's a get-well card for a teacher, so show the teacher | It's a fake birthday card, throw it away | It's a valentine for you, so open it
test: at a visit your friend's step-dad says "your mum and I got together last spring". what does "got together" mean? | They started being a couple | They met at a meeting for work | They had a big fight last spring | They went on a school trip together
exam: Identify the appropriate conduct where an unknown number calls a child's phone. | A parent answers or ignores it, as it may be a scammer | The child answers politely and gives the family's address if it is requested | The child answers and supplies passwords if asked | The child returns the call immediately
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises conduct upon breaking an object at a friend's home? | The adult is informed at once and an apology is offered | The object is hidden so as to avoid any trouble with the adults | The friend is blamed for the breakage | The matter is mentioned only on leaving
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of thank-you notes. | A short note is written to each giver within one or two weeks | A note is written only for costly gifts | A single note is sent to all the givers collectively, once a year | Notes are written only after a year
exam: Ascertain the reason for which scammers telephone children. | To learn when a house is empty or to obtain money and passwords | To offer free gifts that are genuine | To invite children to school events | To check that phones are working
